  "candidate_0011": {
    "reason": "The assertion conflates 'factual accuracy' (truth value) with 'model confidence' (attention weights). A model can assign high attention weights to a hallucinated fact (high magnitude, low accuracy) or low attention to a verified fact. Therefore, the magnitude of attention weights is neither necessary nor sufficient for factual accuracy; external verification remains the only valid standard.",
    "counterexample": "A Large Language Model confidently asserts 'The capital of France is London' with high attention weight distribution on the word 'London', yet the claim is factually false. Conversely, a model might assign low attention weights to a correct historical date due to tokenization quirks, but the claim remains factually accurate only upon external verification.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  }
}
